Yes. That’s a much better definition of what the Pantheon should ultimately produce: not answers, but increasing capability to produce answers.

The old proverb is:

Give someone a fish → solve one instance.
Teach them to fish → give them a reusable capability.

Pantheon goes another level:

\boxed{
\text{Fish}
+\text{Fishing}
+\text{Tools}
+\text{Navigation}
+\text{Adaptation}
+\text{Learning}
}

Because teaching one fishing technique can still overfit one environment.

A stronger system teaches you how to recognize what kind of problem you’re actually facing, choose or construct the appropriate tool, test whether it works, notice when conditions change, preserve the useful lesson, and modify the method next time.

So the output isn’t:

Problem\rightarrow Answer

It’s:

Problem
\rightarrow
\underbrace{\text{Orientation}}_{\text{where am I?}}
\rightarrow
\underbrace{\text{Tool selection}}_{\text{what works here?}}
\rightarrow
\underbrace{\text{Execution}}_{\text{try it}}
\rightarrow
\underbrace{\text{Consequence}}_{\text{what happened?}}
\rightarrow
\underbrace{\text{Learning}}_{\text{why?}}
\rightarrow
\underbrace{\text{Compression}}_{\text{what survives?}}
\rightarrow
\underbrace{\text{Transfer}}_{\text{where else?}}

And “gives you all you’ll need on the way” adds something important.

You don’t necessarily hand the traveler every possible tool before departure. That’s an enormous backpack.

Instead:

\boxed{\text{Kernel}+\text{Map}+\text{Forge}}

Kernel: a small set of primitives that travel well.

Map: ways to recognize terrain and retrieve existing capabilities.

Forge: mechanisms for constructing a missing capability when the map doesn’t contain one.

That third piece matters most.

A finite toolkit eventually encounters something it wasn’t designed for.

A tool-making toolkit can respond:

I don’t have the right tool yet. What properties must such a tool possess?

Then:

\text{Need}
\rightarrow
\text{constraints}
\rightarrow
\text{existing primitives}
\rightarrow
\text{composition}
\rightarrow
\text{candidate tool}
\rightarrow
\text{test}
\rightarrow
\text{retain}

Now every unsolved problem can potentially expand the toolkit that attacks the next one.

That’s where the compounding comes from.

And we should keep one discipline attached to it: Pantheon shouldn’t try to make its user dependent on Pantheon.

A successful interaction should ideally leave behind some portable increase in capability—a distinction, operator, procedure, model, experiment, question, or way of seeing that remains useful even without invoking the entire architecture.

So perhaps the highest-level success metric isn’t:

\text{Did Pantheon answer correctly?}

but:

\boxed{
\text{Did this interaction increase the user's ability to generate,
test, and improve good answers afterward?}
}

Give the fish when someone’s hungry.

Teach fishing when it helps.

Bring the tackle box.

Map the river.

Teach how weather changes the river.

And if someday there aren’t any fish—

teach them how to recognize that they’re solving yesterday’s problem and build a different damn tool. 😏